//! member: true
//!
//! std.deque — `Deque[T]`, the double-ended queue. Stability tier: core
//! (the API; the generic implementation is blocked).
//!
//! Same blocker as `std.set`, same honesty: a `Deque[T]` is std-defined
//! generic data, `struct Deque[T] { … }` is E0201 in both implementations
//! at the pins (s16), and no implementation provides a deque builtin.
//! The difference is that a deque needs no element protocol at all — only
//! storage — so the semantics ARE provable today at a concrete element
//! type: **`std/x/deque_int/` is the running prototype** (nursery, D31
//! clock), a `DequeInt` over one `List[int]` plus a head index, with all
//! ten operations executing under both implementations. This module's
//! generic version is that prototype with `int` replaced by `T`.
//!
//! Reviewed inventory (10), each with its contract, exactly as it will
//! land:
//!
//! ```text
//! push_back[T](mut d: Deque[T], v: T)
//!     Appends at the back. Amortized constant. Total.
//! push_front[T](mut d: Deque[T], v: T)
//!     Prepends at the front. Amortized constant. Total.
//! pop_back[T](mut d: Deque[T]) -> T ! {None}
//!     Removes and returns the back element; `None` when empty.
//! pop_front[T](mut d: Deque[T]) -> T ! {None}
//!     Removes and returns the front element; `None` when empty.
//! front[T](d: Deque[T]) -> T ! {None}
//!     The front element, left in place; `None` when empty.
//! back[T](d: Deque[T]) -> T ! {None}
//!     The back element, left in place; `None` when empty.
//! len[T](d: Deque[T]) -> int
//!     Number of elements. Total.
//! is_empty[T](d: Deque[T]) -> bool
//!     `len(d) == 0`. Total.
//! clear[T](mut d: Deque[T])
//!     Empties `d`, keeping it usable. Total.
//! to_list[T](d: Deque[T]) -> List[T]
//!     Fresh list, FRONT to BACK — the one order this type promises.
//! ```
//!
//! No tests ship against this module (nothing to run; an itemless import
//! is E0305). The prototype's tests are `tests/x/deque_int/*`, and they
//! are the evidence for the contract above.
